# The identity and credentials `build.sh --notarize` needs, so that they are written down once instead of
# being looked up in Xcode and App Store Connect every time.
#
# Copy this to `notarize.env` beside it and fill it in. That file is ignored by git: the API key it points
# at is a credential, and the identity and the issuer are account details that do not belong in a public
# repository. `build.sh` reads it if it is there and works without it if it is not.
#
#   cp installer/macos/notarize.env.example installer/macos/notarize.env

# The signing identity, exactly as `security find-identity -v -p codesigning` prints it. The part in
# brackets is the Team ID.
CODESIGN_IDENTITY="Developer ID Application: Your Name (TEAMID)"

# The keychain profile notarytool submits as. `build.sh` stores it for you the first time from the three
# values below, and after that this is the only one it needs.
NOTARY_PROFILE=unluminate

# An App Store Connect API key, which is what creates that profile. From appstoreconnect.apple.com,
# Users and Access, Integrations, App Store Connect API:
#   NOTARY_KEY       the AuthKey_<keyid>.p8 file you downloaded. Keep it outside the repository.
#   NOTARY_KEY_ID    the ten characters in the key's row, which are also in the file's name
#   NOTARY_ISSUER    the Issuer ID above the table, a UUID, the same for every key on the team
# A path relative to the repository works too: the file is read by the script, which sets $repo.
NOTARY_KEY="$HOME/private/keys/AuthKey_XXXXXXXXXX.p8"
NOTARY_KEY_ID=XXXXXXXXXX
NOTARY_ISSUER=00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000

# An app-specific password from appleid.apple.com works instead of an API key. Use one or the other.
# NOTARY_APPLE_ID=you@example.com
# NOTARY_TEAM_ID=TEAMID
# NOTARY_PASSWORD=xxxx-xxxx-xxxx-xxxx
